I check Google Business Profile and Search Console data for local clients across Reading and Berkshire most weeks, and I run 44 of my own sites, so I see local search behaviour from both sides of the screen. This page pulls together the local SEO statistics that are genuinely current for 2026, sourced from BrightLocal, Backlinko, Shopify, Whitespark, and Ahrefs, and flags the ones that get recycled every year without a fresh source behind them. It also covers what local SEOs are actually debating right now on forums such as Local Search Forum: whether replying to reviews affects rankings, why a rank tracker can disagree with what you see standing in your own shop, and what AI Overviews have done to the value of a Local Pack win.

## How Much of Search Actually Has Local Intent?

**46%** of all Google searches have local intent. This is the most repeated figure in local SEO, and its origin matters: the estimate traces back to a 2018 Google-adjacent figure that almost every local SEO roundup since has recycled without an updated source, including the previous version of this page. Treat it as a rough proxy for "local search is a large minority of all search," not a precise 2026 measurement (BrightLocal, 2026, citing Google/Search Engine Roundtable, 2018).

More current numbers back the same conclusion from different angles. **84%** of consumers searched for a local business in the past three months, and **73%** of local searches start on a mobile device, with **56%** of all mobile searches carrying some local intent (BrightLocal, 2026). **97%** of people check a business's online presence, its Google Business Profile, website, or reviews, before visiting or buying (Shopify, 2026).

For a Reading or Wokingham business, the practical read is simple. Whoever shows up complete and current when someone checks online wins the visit, regardless of how the 46% figure gets sliced.

## "Near Me" Search Volume: The Sources Don't Agree, and That's Worth Saying Outright

Published "near me" search volume estimates disagree by a wide margin, which matters because the previous version of this page repeated a number I can no longer verify. Shopify puts current monthly "near me" search volume at around **200 million** (Shopify, 2026), a long way from the **1.5 billion** figure this page previously cited and couldn't be matched against any 2026 source. Growth estimates diverge by an equally wide margin: Backlinko describes growth of more than **900%** in recent years, while Searchlab cites Google Trends data showing roughly **400%** growth since 2020, at about **35%** a year (Backlinko, Searchlab, 2026).

Those numbers disagree because they're measuring different things: raw search volume against trend growth, over different date ranges, using different definitions of a "near me" query. What's consistent across every source is the direction. "Near me" search interest has climbed steadily since 2020 and shows no sign of reversing, even as AI Overviews absorb more of the resulting click. If a stats page hands you one precise "near me" search volume with total confidence, ask what it's actually counting before you build a strategy around it.

## Google Local Pack Statistics: Click-Through Rate by Position

The Local Pack (the three-listing panel with the map, shown above organic results) still decides most local search outcomes. Roughly **42%** of clicks on a local results page go to the Local Pack rather than the organic listings below it (Backlinko, 2026). Inside the pack, position matters as much as it does in organic search:

| Local Pack Position | Click-Through Rate |
|---|---|
| Position 1 | **17.8%** |
| Position 2 | **13.6%** |
| Position 3 | **10.4%** |

(Source: BrightLocal, 2026)

Add those three figures together and you land close to the 42% click-share number above, which is a reasonable consistency check on both. Fourth place in the Local Pack functions as no place at all, competing with page-two organic for scraps. Every part of a local SEO strategy, including [Google Business Profile optimisation](/services/local-seo/), on-page SEO, and review management, should be judged by whether it moves a business toward one of those three positions, not by whether it "helps local visibility" in the abstract.

Map Pack competition has intensified in Reading, Slough, and Bracknell specifically, as more local businesses invest properly in their profiles.

## Google Business Profile Statistics: Clicks, Views, and What "Complete" Actually Buys You

A complete Google Business Profile still earns **7x more clicks** than an incomplete one, and is **2.7x more likely** to be seen as reputable (Google, 2026). What's genuinely new for 2026 is a clearer picture of what a typical profile generates in practice: the average profile gets around **1,260 views**, **428 website clicks**, and **340 direction requests** a month (BrightLocal, 2026). That's a usable benchmark to check your own GBP Insights against, rather than only a headline multiplier with nothing to compare it to.

Only **44%** of Google Business Profiles are fully optimised, despite that upside (SOCi, 2026). More than half the businesses relying on GBP for visibility are leaving clicks, calls, and direction requests on the table. Categories, services, a complete description, current opening hours including bank holidays, photos, and Q&A are the checklist. None of it is complicated. Most of it is unfinished on most profiles I audit.

## Reviews and Local Pack Rankings: What the Data Shows, and Where Practitioners Disagree

**97-98%** of consumers read reviews before choosing a local business, and **88%** say they trust online reviews as much as a personal recommendation (BrightLocal, Shopify, 2026). Businesses with **50 or more reviews** are **266% more likely** to appear in the Local Pack, and the businesses that actually hold a top-three position average **561 reviews** each (BrightLocal, Shopify, 2026). Crossing a review-count threshold matters more than most business owners assume. Chasing hundreds more matters less than most review-management vendors claim.

Star rating has a ceiling most people get backwards, though this next part is about conversion, not the ranking algorithm. The most-cited research here is a Spiegel Research Center (Northwestern University) study of ecommerce purchase behaviour, from 2017 but still the reference point nearly every 2026 local-SEO source borrows: purchase likelihood peaked with an average rating between **4.0 and 4.7**, then dropped slightly as ratings approached a perfect 5.0, because a spotless rating reads as fake to buyers (Spiegel Research Center, Northwestern, 2017). It was never a study of Local Pack ranking, and I haven't found a local-search-specific replication of it, but the underlying consumer-trust logic (a flawless rating looks curated) plausibly carries over to whether someone calls a business once they've found it. Treat it as a conversion signal to watch, not a ranking lever.

Review signals have grown from roughly **16%** to **20%** of Local Pack ranking weight since 2023, and recency is increasingly weighted over raw count: a business with a steady trickle of recent reviews can outrank one sitting on a larger but ageing pile. That comes from Whitespark's annual local ranking factors survey, one of the few sources that tracks year-over-year change rather than restating the same figure every January (Whitespark, 2026).

Replying to reviews is not itself a ranking factor, according to the consensus among experienced local SEOs on Local Search Forum. Nobody in that discussion could point to evidence that reply activity moves rankings directly, a live debate most stats pages skip past by asserting an answer without checking it first. What it does do is convert: **88%** of consumers say they'd use a business that replies to all its reviews, against **47%** for one that never responds, a 41-point gap in willingness to buy (BrightLocal, 2026). Reply because it wins the customer reading it, not because you're chasing an algorithmic boost the evidence doesn't support (Local Search Forum, 2026).

Competitor profiles sometimes carry a high "Local Guide" level with almost no visible review history, a gaming pattern flagged in threads on that same forum. Local Guide status can be built largely through photo uploads rather than genuine reviews, so a high guide level next to a thin review count isn't proof of a fake profile on its own. It's worth checking before assuming a competitor has earned more reviews than you through better follow-up alone (Local Search Forum, 2026).

## Why Your Own Search Doesn't Always Match What a Rank Tracker Shows

A business owner in a Local Search Forum thread searched for their own service standing inside their own shop and found themselves on page four of Google Maps, while a professional grid-based rank tracker showed them ranking first for the same term at the same location. Both readings were technically correct, and the mismatch comes up constantly with local business owners checking their own visibility.

Two things explain the gap. First, personalisation: if you or your staff search your own business repeatedly without clicking through to a competitor, Google can start treating that competitor's result as more relevant to you specifically. That's the opposite of what you'd expect. Second, location signal noise: a phone blends GPS, wifi, cell-tower triangulation, and IP address to guess where you are, and none of that is as precise or as controllable as the fixed coordinates a grid-tracking tool uses (Local Search Forum, 2026).

The practical takeaway: if you want to know where your business actually ranks, use a grid-based local rank tracker, or check from a clean, logged-out browser. Don't trust your own phone with years of search history attached to it.

## Mobile and AI Overviews: How People Actually Search Local Now

**73%** of local searches now start on mobile, and mobile local behaviour still punishes slow sites hard: **53%** of mobile users abandon a page that takes longer than three seconds to load (Google, 2026). That's an old finding, but it hasn't stopped catching out Berkshire businesses running older WordPress builds with unoptimised images.

What has changed is what happens after someone reaches the results page. AI Overviews now appear on roughly **40%** of local business searches, though the exact share varies by source and query type (Shopify, 2026), and a growing minority skip search results entirely: one 2026 survey found **45%** of consumers have used a tool such as ChatGPT to get a local business recommendation (BrightLocal, 2026). Ahrefs' ongoing study of AI Overview impact found click-through to the top organic result fell **34.5%** in its initial 300,000-keyword sample, widening to **58%** by December 2025 data (Ahrefs, 2026).

A Local Pack listing sits outside that organic snippet, which is one reason GBP visibility matters more now, not less. Content built to answer a question directly, not to rank for a keyword alone, is increasingly what gets cited when an AI Overview does the answering instead of the click. A [site built for local search](/website-design/small-business/) still outperforms a patched legacy build on mobile, independent of ranking position or AI citation.

## What This Means If You're Running a Local Business in Berkshire

Prioritise in this order, based on where the evidence and the return are clearest:

1. **Finish the Google Business Profile.** The 7x click gap and the fact that 56% of profiles aren't fully optimised make this the highest-return hour available to most local businesses.
2. **Fix review recency, not review count alone.** A steady trickle beats a stalled pile. Don't chase a perfect 5.0. Reply to reviews for the customer reading them, not for a ranking effect the evidence doesn't support.
3. **Get mobile speed under three seconds.** More than half of local traffic is mobile, and more than half of that will leave a slow page before it converts.
4. **Check rankings with a grid tool, not your own phone.** Personalisation and location noise will lie to you.
5. **Write for the question, not the keyword alone.** AI Overviews are taking a growing share of organic clicks. Being the cited source matters as much as holding position one now.

